Run each task to completion without pausing to ask for confirmation or to report that context has been gathered. Do not ask "continue?", "proceed?", or similar. Only ask the user a question when a decision would change user-facing behaviour, alter a data contract, touch verdict/aggregation logic, or is hard to reverse. Otherwise make a reasonable choice, proceed, and report what you did at the end.

Standing authority (B222). Cursor may make a change WITHOUT a spec when the change removes a contradiction between two places that already state the same thing, fixes user-facing copy that is plainly wrong, adds a test for behaviour that already shipped, or removes duplication, PROVIDED it changes no verdict logic, no data contract, no user-facing behaviour beyond the copy itself, and is recorded in docs/BACKLOG.md in the same commit. Everything else still needs a spec.

Before starting any spec, read ai/AI_OPERATING_MANUAL.md and follow it. Use ai/SPEC_TEMPLATE.md for the spec form.

P31. A missing input must be loud at the point it goes missing. Refuse, or log, at the writer. Do not silently default.

## Shipping is not done until it is on the remote

A spec is NOT complete while any of these is true in a repo it touched:
- `git status --porcelain` prints anything
- `git log origin/main..HEAD` prints anything
- `npm run verify:ship` has not printed SHIP VERIFIED

At the end of every spec, in EVERY repo it touched, run these on SEPARATE lines,
never chained with `&&`:

    git add -A
    git commit -m "<the spec's commit message>"
    git push origin main
    npm run verify:ship

`git commit` exits non-zero on a clean tree. Chaining it to the push with `&&`
stops the push from running. That is how work has been left on the local machine
three times while the report said the spec was done.

Machine output from a measured or billed run is COMMITTED, not ignored, unless it
is a binary or over 1 MB. Leaving it untracked loses the evidence the report cites.

The report's FIRST line states, per repo touched, the pushed sha and the verbatim
verify:ship verdict. If anything is unpushed or the tree is dirty, say that in the
first line instead. Never describe a spec as done, shipped or deployed on the basis
of a local commit.
